Typed Institutional–Causal Governance Model: 

General Exposure Law, Information-Theoretic Independence, and Child-Structural Mechanism Design

Ouadi Maakoul & SI TEAM

1. Introduction

This paper develops a formal governance model for algorithmic policies affecting children. The central architectural problem is that formal authority separation — the structural constraint that no epistemic agent writes the institutional decision — is definitionally true and carries no empirical content. We replace it with a quantified exposure law that measures how much influence epistemic agents exert on institutional decisions even when they hold no formal authority.

The model proceeds in four stages. First, we establish a Gaussian–linear exposure law grounded in Fisher information and the standard design-effect correction. Second, we generalize to arbitrary loss and error structures using Bayes risk and conditional mutual information. Third, we prove three impossibility results: multidimensional developmental aggregation (Arrow–Raynaud), epistemic Sybil resistance, and the accountability horizon under compound autonomy. Fourth, we make the child domain structural through sensitive-period constraints, evolving-capacity bounds, and multi-principal mechanism design.

