4. General Exposure Law: Information-Theoretic and Bayes-Risk Framework

4.1 Information-theoretic effective independence

Definition 4.1 (Information Redundancy Index).

\mathcal{I}_I(E) \triangleq \frac{I(e_1, \dots, e_k; \Theta)}{\sum_{a=1}^k I(e_a; \Theta)}.

Definition 4.2 (Information-Theoretic Effective Sample Size).

\boxed{n_{\text{eff}}^I \triangleq k \cdot \mathcal{I}_I(E) = \frac{k \cdot I(e_1, \dots, e_k; \Theta)}{\sum_{a=1}^k I(e_a; \Theta)}.}

Proposition 4.1 (Concordance with design-effect formula). Under the equicorrelated Gaussian model, as the signal-to-noise ratio \mathrm{SNR} = \sigma_\Theta^2 / \sigma^2 \to 0,

\lim_{\mathrm{SNR} \to 0} n_{\text{eff}}^I = \frac{k}{1 + (k-1)\rho} = n_{\text{eff}}.

Proof. Under the equicorrelated Gaussian model, I(E; \Theta) = \frac{\mathrm{SNR}}{2} \mathbf{1}^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_\varepsilon^{-1} \mathbf{1} + \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{SNR}^2). For \mathbf{\Sigma}_\varepsilon = \sigma^2 ((1-\rho)\mathbf{I} + \rho \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^T), \mathbf{1}^T \mathbf{\Sigma}_\varepsilon^{-1} \mathbf{1} = k / (\sigma^2 [1 + (k-1)\rho]). Substituting yields the result. \blacksquare

4.2 Bayes risk exposure fraction

Definition 4.3 (Risk Baselines).

R_H^* \triangleq \inf_{d_H: \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{H}, \Theta}[L(d_H(h), \Theta)]

R_E^* \triangleq \inf_{d_E: \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{E}, \Theta}[L(d_E(E), \Theta)]

R_{\text{joint}}^* \triangleq \inf_{d: \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{H}, \mathcal{E}, \Theta}[L(d(h, E), \Theta)]

Definition 4.4 (Bayes Risk Exposure Fraction). For a well-calibrated \delta satisfying R_{\text{joint}}^* \le R_\delta \le R_H^*,

\boxed{\alpha_{\text{BR}} \triangleq \frac{R_H^* - R_\delta}{R_H^* - R_{\text{joint}}^*}.}

· \alpha_{\text{BR}} = 0: Pure human governance.
· \alpha_{\text{BR}} = 1: Optimal epistemic integration.
· \alpha_{\text{BR}} < 0: Miscalibrated deference.

4.3 Mutual information exposure index

Definition 4.5.

\boxed{\alpha_I \triangleq \frac{I(D; E \mid h)}{I(D; E, h)}.}

Properties. 0 \le \alpha_I \le 1. \alpha_I = 0 \iff D \perp\!\!\!\perp E \mid h. \alpha_I = 1 \iff D \perp\!\!\!\perp h \mid E. Invariant under bijective transformations.

4.4 General bound

Theorem 4.1 (Information Bottleneck Bound). For any \delta: \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{E} \to \Delta(\mathcal{D}),

I(D; \Theta \mid h) \le I(E; \Theta \mid h) \le I(E; \Theta) = \frac{n_{\text{eff}}^I}{k} \sum_{a=1}^k I(e_a; \Theta).

Proof. By the Data Processing Inequality on \Theta \to (h, E) \to D, I(D; \Theta \mid h) \le I(E; \Theta \mid h). Conditional mutual information is bounded by unconditioned mutual information. Applying Definition 4.2 yields the result. \blacksquare

Corollary 4.1 (General separation failure). Formal authority separation does not bound \alpha_I or \alpha_{\text{BR}}.

