6. Second-Order Uncertainty and Non-Collapsible Decision Rules

6.1 The \kappa-measurability condition

Let \pi_1: \Delta(\Delta(\mathcal R)) \to \Delta(\mathcal R) be the barycenter map:

\pi_1(\nu) \triangleq \int_{\Delta(\mathcal R)} p \, d\nu(p).

Definition 6.1 (\kappa-measurable decision rule). An institutional decision rule \delta: \Delta(\Delta(\mathcal R)) \to \Delta(\mathcal D) satisfies \kappa-measurability if and only if \delta is not measurable with respect to \sigma(\pi_1):

\boxed{\delta \notin \mathcal M\bigl( \sigma(\pi_1) \bigr).}

Equivalently, there exist \nu_1, \nu_2 \in \Delta(\Delta(\mathcal R)) such that \pi_1(\nu_1) = \pi_1(\nu_2) but \delta(\nu_1) \neq \delta(\nu_2).

6.2 Credal set foundations

Because fully specified second-order measures are generally unidentifiable from finite data, the framework operationalizes epistemic states using credal sets \mathcal N \subseteq \Delta(\mathcal R): non-empty, closed, convex sets of candidate first-order distributions.

6.3 Ambiguity-sensitive decision rules

\Gamma-maximin (Gilboa–Schmeidler).

\boxed{\delta_{\Gamma}(E) \triangleq \arg\min_{d \in \mathcal D} \sup_{p \in \mathcal N} \mathbb{E}_p \bigl[ L(d, \mathbf R) \bigr].}

Proposition 6.1 (\Gamma-maximin satisfies \kappa-measurability). Let \mathcal N_1, \mathcal N_2 have identical centroids \pi_1(\mathcal N_1) = \pi_1(\mathcal N_2) = p_0 but distinct convex hulls with \mathcal N_1 \subset \mathcal N_2. Then \delta_{\Gamma}(\mathcal N_1) \neq \delta_{\Gamma}(\mathcal N_2) for non-constant loss. \blacksquare

Smooth ambiguity (Klibanoff–Marinacci–Mukerji). The KMM model introduces second-order beliefs about probability distributions. The individual computes expected utility under each distribution, applies a nonlinear transformation \phi reflecting ambiguity attitude, and averages across distributions using second-order beliefs .

\boxed{\delta_{\text{KMM}}(\nu) \triangleq \arg\max_{d \in \mathcal D} \int_{\Delta(\mathcal R)} \phi\left( \mathbb{E}_p \bigl[ u(d, \mathbf R) \bigr] \right) d\nu(p).}

Theorem 6.1 (Moment separation under KMM). Let \phi be strictly concave. Then \delta_{\text{KMM}} depends on the second-order variance of expected utility across candidate risk models.

Proof. Applying a second-order Taylor expansion:

\int \phi(v_p) \, d\nu(p) = \phi(\bar{v}) + \frac{1}{2} \phi''(\bar{v}) \operatorname{Var}_{\nu}(v_p) + \mathcal{O}\left( \mathbb{E}_\nu \bigl[ (v_p - \bar{v})^3 \bigr] \right).

Because \phi''(\bar{v}) < 0, the objective penalizes \operatorname{Var}_\nu(v_p). Two states with equal \bar{v} but unequal prediction variances yield distinct evaluations. \blacksquare

6.4 Governance reporting

The institution reports (\mathcal N_t, \hat{\mathbf R}_t, \Gamma_t, \alpha_I, \kappa_{\text{status}}), where \mathcal N_t is the credal set, \hat{\mathbf R}_t \in \mathcal F_{\text{Pareto}}, \Gamma_t \in \{\text{Maximin}, \text{KMM}_\phi\}, \alpha_I is the mutual information exposure index, and \kappa_{\text{status}} \in \{0,1\} verifies \delta(\mathcal N_t) \neq \delta(\pi_1(\mathcal N_t)).

