7. Making the Child Domain Structural

7.1 Sensitive periods and irreversible developmental losses

Definition 7.1 (Sensitive Period Loss Function).

L(d, \mathbf R, t) \triangleq \sum_{j=1}^6 w_j(t) R_j(d),

where

\boxed{w_j(t) \triangleq w_{0,j} + \gamma_j \cdot \mathbb{I}(t \in T_{s,j}) \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{(t - t_{s,j}^*)^2}{2\sigma_j^2} \right).}

Sensitive periods are windows of heightened plasticity during which experience has maximal impact. Critical periods are characterized by enhanced sensitivity restricted to a sharply defined time period such that effects are irreversible; sensitive periods show plasticity both before and after, and exposure during sensitive windows is not necessarily irreversible .

Definition 7.2 (Hard Irreversibility Barrier). For critical periods, the institutional decision map enforces:

\boxed{w_j(t) \triangleq \infty \quad \text{if } t \in T_{s,j} \text{ and } \mathbb{P}\bigl(g_j(t) < g_{j,\text{crit}} \mid d\bigr) > \epsilon_{\text{safety}}.}

For sensitive (non-irreversible) periods, a large finite penalty \gamma_j \gg 0 is appropriate.

7.2 Evolving capacities and sovereign ceilings

Definition 7.3 (Evolving Capacity Function). Let a(t): [0, t_{\max}] \to [0, 1] be continuous, non-decreasing, and measurable:

\frac{d a(t)}{d t} \ge 0, \quad a(0) = 0, \quad a(t_{\max}) = 1.

A parametric realization is the sigmoidal curve:

a(t) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left( -\beta (t - t_{\text{trans}}) \right)}.

CRC General Comment No. 14, para. 44 states that the evolving capacities of the child must be taken into consideration when the child's best interests and right to be heard are at stake; as the child matures, their views shall have increasing weight in the assessment of their best interests .

Definition 7.4 (Dynamic Participation Weighting).

\delta(h, E, \text{Voice}_c, t) = (1 - a(t)) \cdot \delta_{\text{inst}}(h, E) + a(t) \cdot \delta_{\text{child}}(\text{Voice}_c).

Definition 7.5 (External Sovereign Ceiling Constraint). As the child's capacity grows, external paternalistic authority must strictly shrink:

\boxed{\operatorname{Authority}_{\text{ext}}(t) \triangleq \sup_{\sigma_{\text{ext}}} \mathrm{TV}\left( \delta(\cdot) \;\big\vert{}\; \text{control} \sim \sigma_{\text{ext}} \right) \le 1 - a(t).}

As t \to t_{\max} (a(t) \to 1), \operatorname{Authority}_{\text{ext}}(t) \to 0, rendering paternalistic override structurally impossible in the limit.

7.3 Multi-principal mechanism design

The child is not a unitary principal. Let

\mathcal P=\{c,g,p,i\}

denote child, guardian, platform, and institution. Their interests may conflict.

Definition 7.6 (Multi-Principal Mechanism Formulation).

\delta: \mathcal E \times \mathcal H_I \times \mathcal S_c \times \mathcal S_g \times \mathcal S_p \to \Delta(\mathcal D),

where \mathcal S_c, \mathcal S_g, \mathcal S_p are the strategy/reporting spaces of child, guardian, and platform respectively.

Proposition 7.1 (Platform–Guardian Collusion and Autonomy Suppression). In the absence of structural rights constraints, any standard Bayesian mechanism \delta designed as a Pareto-efficient compromise between platform (p) and guardian (g) strictly collapses child autonomy A(t).

Proof. Let U_{pg}(\lambda_p) = \lambda_p U_p(d) + (1-\lambda_p) U_g(d) with \lambda_p \in (0, 1). Platform utility increases in session length and friction-free interaction. Guardian utility increases in immediate safety and behavioral control. Optimization of U_{pg} selects policies minimizing frictional child resistance while maximizing paternalistic restriction:

\arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi} U_{pg}(\pi) \implies A(t) \to 0.

Because child autonomy yields negative short-term utility to g and introduces friction for p, bilateral alignment strictly suppresses A(t). \blacksquare

Structural Remedy: Inalienable Child Rights Constraints. The decision map must impose rights boundaries \mathcal C_{\text{rights}}(t) that cannot be overridden by platform optimization or guardian consent:

\boxed{\delta(h, E, \mathbf{s}) = \arg\min_{d \in \mathcal D_{\text{admissible}}} U_i(d) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad d \in \mathcal C_{\text{rights}}(t)}

where

\mathcal C_{\text{rights}}(t) \triangleq \left\{ d \in \mathcal D \;\middle\vert{}\; A(t; d) \ge a(t) \cdot A_{\text{target}}, \; \mathbb{P}\bigl(g_j(t) < g_{j,\text{crit}} \mid d\bigr) \le \epsilon_{\text{safety}} \right\}.

