Appendix B: Fisher Information for Equicorrelated Errors

B.1 Information from the full vector

Under Gaussian errors, the log-likelihood of E = (e_1, \dots, e_k) given \Theta is

\ell(\Theta; E) = -\tfrac{1}{2} (E - \Theta \mathbf{1})^\top \Sigma^{-1} (E - \Theta \mathbf{1}) + \text{const}.

The Fisher information is

J(E) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left( \frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \Theta} \right)^2 \right] = \mathbf{1}^\top \Sigma^{-1} \mathbf{1}.

B.2 Inverse of the equicorrelated covariance

Write \Sigma = \sigma^2 (1-\rho)[I + \frac{\rho}{1-\rho} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top]. By the Sherman–Morrison formula,

\Sigma^{-1} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2(1-\rho)} \left[ I - \frac{\rho}{1+(k-1)\rho} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \right].

Then

\mathbf{1}^\top \Sigma^{-1} \mathbf{1} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2(1-\rho)} \left[ k - \frac{\rho k^2}{1+(k-1)\rho} \right].

Factoring,

\mathbf{1}^\top \Sigma^{-1} \mathbf{1} = \frac{k}{\sigma^2(1-\rho)} \cdot \frac{1-\rho}{1+(k-1)\rho} = \frac{k}{\sigma^2[1+(k-1)\rho]}.

Thus

J(E) = \frac{k}{\sigma^2[1+(k-1)\rho]}.

B.3 Individual information and the independence ratio

For any single agent,

J(e_a) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}, \qquad \sum_a J(e_a) = \frac{k}{\sigma^2}.

Therefore

\mathcal{I}(E) = \frac{J(E)}{\sum_a J(e_a)} = \frac{1}{1+(k-1)\rho},

and

n_{\text{eff}} = k \, \mathcal{I}(E) = \frac{k}{1+(k-1)\rho}. \quad \blacksquare

---

Appendix C: Information-Theoretic Effective Sample Size

C.1 Mutual information in the Gaussian model

Let \Theta \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\Theta^2) and E \mid \Theta \sim \mathcal{N}(\Theta \mathbf{1}, \Sigma). Then

I(E; \Theta) = h(E) - h(E \mid \Theta) = \tfrac{1}{2} \log \frac{\det(\Sigma + \sigma_\Theta^2 \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top)}{\det(\Sigma)}.

By the matrix determinant lemma, \det(\Sigma + \sigma_\Theta^2 \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top) = \det(\Sigma)(1 + \sigma_\Theta^2 \mathbf{1}^\top \Sigma^{-1} \mathbf{1}). Hence

I(E; \Theta) = \tfrac{1}{2} \log \left( 1 + \mathrm{SNR} \cdot \frac{k}{1+(k-1)\rho} \right),

where \mathrm{SNR} = \sigma_\Theta^2 / \sigma^2.

C.2 Single-agent mutual information

For a single agent e_a = \Theta + \varepsilon_a,

I(e_a; \Theta) = \tfrac{1}{2} \log(1 + \mathrm{SNR}).

C.3 Low-SNR limit

Using \log(1+x) = x + \mathcal{O}(x^2) as x \to 0,

I(E; \Theta) = \frac{\mathrm{SNR}}{2} \cdot \frac{k}{1+(k-1)\rho} + \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{SNR}^2),

I(e_a; \Theta) = \frac{\mathrm{SNR}}{2} + \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{SNR}^2).

Therefore

n_{\text{eff}}^I = \frac{k \cdot I(E; \Theta)}{\sum_a I(e_a; \Theta)} = \frac{k \cdot \frac{k}{1+(k-1)\rho}}{k} \cdot (1 + \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{SNR})) = \frac{k}{1+(k-1)\rho} + \mathcal{O}(\mathrm{SNR}).

Letting \mathrm{SNR} \to 0 yields n_{\text{eff}}^I \to n_{\text{eff}}. \blacksquare

