Appendix E: Mutual Information Exposure Index

E.1 Definition

Let D \sim \delta(h, E) denote the institutional decision variable and

\alpha_I \triangleq \frac{I(D; E \mid h)}{I(D; E, h)}.

E.2 Range and invariance

Range. I(D; E \mid h) \le I(D; E, h) by the chain rule and non-negativity of mutual information:

I(D; E, h) = I(D; h) + I(D; E \mid h) \ge I(D; E \mid h).

Thus 0 \le \alpha_I \le 1.

Boundary cases.

· \alpha_I = 0 \iff I(D; E \mid h) = 0 \iff D \perp E \mid h: complete epistemic insulation.
· \alpha_I = 1 \iff I(D; h) = 0 \iff D \perp h \mid E: complete epistemic takeover by agents.

Bijective invariance. If \phi_D, \phi_h, \phi_E are bijections, mutual information is preserved: I(\phi_D(D); \phi_E(E) \mid \phi_h(h)) = I(D; E \mid h). Hence \alpha_I is invariant under bijective reparameterization.

E.3 Consistency with the variance-share exposure

In the linear-Gaussian model with D = (1-\lambda)h + \lambda \bar{e}, the joint distribution of (D, h, E) is Gaussian. By the standard identity for Gaussian mutual information,

I(D; E \mid h) = \tfrac{1}{2} \log \left( 1 + \frac{\lambda^2 \sigma^2 / n_{\text{eff}}}{(1-\lambda)^2 \tau^2} \right),

I(D; h) = \tfrac{1}{2} \log \left( 1 + \frac{(1-\lambda)^2 \tau^2}{\lambda^2 \sigma^2 / n_{\text{eff}}} \right).

Since I(D; E, h) = I(D; h) + I(D; E \mid h), the ratio \alpha_I is a monotone transform of the signal-to-noise ratio \lambda^2 s / [(1-\lambda)^2 \tau^2]. In the low-SNR regime this transform is approximately linear, so \alpha_I tracks the variance-share exposure \alpha up to first order in the SNR. Exact equality holds only in the degenerate limit.

E.4 Data processing bound

Theorem E.1. For any \delta: \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{E} \to \Delta(\mathcal{D}) and any state variable \Theta satisfying the Markov chain \Theta \to (h, E) \to D,

I(D; \Theta \mid h) \le I(E; \Theta \mid h) \le I(E; \Theta).

Proof. The first inequality follows from the data processing inequality applied to the channel (h, E) \to D conditioned on h. The second follows from the fact that conditioning on h cannot increase mutual information when h carries no additional information beyond E about \Theta. \blacksquare

