Appendix F: Arrow–Raynaud Impossibility

F.1 Formal setup

Let X be a finite set of policies with |X| \ge 3. Let N = \{1, \dots, n\} be a finite set of dimensions, with n = 6 in the developmental application. Each dimension j induces a weak order \succsim_j on X. A social welfare function is a map

F: \prod_{j=1}^n \mathcal{W}(X) \to \mathcal{W}(X),

where \mathcal{W}(X) denotes the set of weak orders on X.

F.2 Axioms

Weak Pareto (WP). For all x, y \in X, if x \succ_j y for all j \in N, then x \succ_F y.

Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA). For all x, y \in X and all profiles (\succsim_j), (\succsim_j'),

(x \succsim_j y \iff x \succsim_j' y \ \forall j) \implies (x \succsim_F y \iff x \succsim_F' y).

Non-Dictatorship (ND). There is no j^* \in N such that for all profiles and all x, y \in X,

x \succ_{j^*} y \implies x \succ_F y.

Ordinal Non-Comparability (ONC). For any profile (\succsim_j) and any collection of strictly increasing maps \phi_j: X \to \mathbb{R}, the social order F(\succsim_1, \dots, \succsim_n) is unchanged when each \succsim_j is replaced by the order induced by \phi_j \circ u_j, where u_j represents \succsim_j.

F.3 Arrow's theorem

Theorem F.1 (Arrow, 1951). If |X| \ge 3 and F satisfies WP, IIA, and ONC, then F is dictatorial: there exists j^* such that F coincides with \succsim_{j^*} on every profile.

F.4 Application

Identifying dimensions j \in \{L, C, W, S, P, A\} with voters and policies \pi \in \Pi with alternatives, Theorem F.1 applies directly. Thus no F satisfies WP, IIA, ND, and ONC simultaneously. \blacksquare

F.5 Weighted sum as a boundary case

The weighted sum F(\mathbf{G}) = \sum_j w_j G_j satisfies WP and ND but uses cardinal comparability: it is not invariant under independent strictly increasing transformations \phi_j. It therefore violates ONC, consistent with Theorem F.1.

