Appendix G: Epistemic Sybil Barrier

G.1 Setup

Let \Theta \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\Theta^2). Agents a_1, a_2 produce reports e_1, e_2. Let \phi: \mathcal{A}_E \to \mathcal{R}_{\text{anc}} be the root-attribution map.

Case A (independence). \phi(a_1) \neq \phi(a_2). Then \varepsilon_1 \perp \varepsilon_2, and the joint likelihood is

p(e_1, e_2 \mid \Theta) = \mathcal{N}(e_1; \Theta, \sigma^2) \cdot \mathcal{N}(e_2; \Theta, \sigma^2).

Posterior precision:

\operatorname{Prec}_A(\Theta \mid e_1, e_2) = \frac{1}{\sigma_\Theta^2} + \frac{2}{\sigma^2}.

Case B (Sybil duplication). \phi(a_1) = \phi(a_2). Then \varepsilon_2 = \varepsilon_1 almost surely, and

p(e_1, e_2 \mid \Theta) = \mathcal{N}(e_1; \Theta, \sigma^2) \cdot \delta(e_2 - e_1).

Posterior precision:

\operatorname{Prec}_B(\Theta \mid e_1, e_2) = \frac{1}{\sigma_\Theta^2} + \frac{1}{\sigma^2}.

G.2 Impossibility

Theorem G.1. No report-only aggregator \delta_E: \mathcal{E}^k \to \Delta(\Theta) can be Bayes-correct in both Case A and Case B.

Proof. Suppose \delta_E is Bayes-correct in Case A. Then for any (e_1, e_2), the posterior \delta_E(e_1, e_2) has precision 1/\sigma_\Theta^2 + 2/\sigma^2. But in Case B, the same numerical input (e_1, e_2) is generated with posterior precision 1/\sigma_\Theta^2 + 1/\sigma^2. The two precisions differ, so \delta_E cannot be Bayes-correct in Case B. The symmetric argument holds if \delta_E is Bayes-correct in Case B. \blacksquare

G.3 Sybil invariance

Definition G.1. \delta_E is Sybil-invariant if for every report vector E = (e_1, \dots, e_k) and every duplication E' = (e_1, \dots, e_k, \underbrace{e_a, \dots, e_a}_{m}), \delta_E(E') = \delta_E(E).

Corollary G.1. No \delta_E can be simultaneously Sybil-invariant and Bayes-correct across all root partitions \phi.

Proof. Sybil invariance forces \delta_E to treat duplicated reports identically to a single report. Bayes correctness in Case A requires the opposite (each independent report increases precision). Therefore the two requirements are mutually exclusive. \blacksquare

