Appendix H: Accountability Horizon

H.1 Setup

Let G = (V, E_{\text{causal}}) be a directed graph with a directed cycle C \subseteq G. Let \mathbf{S}_t denote the joint system state. Define the feedback transfer operator \mathcal{T}_C by

\mathcal{T}_C: \mathbf{S}_t \mapsto \mathbf{S}_{t+\Delta t}.

The compound autonomy index is

\Lambda \triangleq \rho\left( \frac{\partial \mathbf{S}_{t+\Delta t}}{\partial \mathbf{S}_t} \right),

the spectral radius of the Jacobian of the feedback map.

H.2 Threshold

Theorem H.1 (Tibebu, 2026). Let |C_{\min}| denote the size of the minimum compound autonomy set in G. Define

\hat{\Lambda}^{*} = 1 - \frac{1}{|C_{\min}|}.

If \Lambda > \hat{\Lambda}^{*} and G contains a human–AI feedback cycle, then no single-locus accountability framework satisfies Attributability, Foreseeability, Non-Vacuity, and Completeness simultaneously.

H.3 Proof sketch

As \Lambda \to 1, the Jacobian \partial \mathbf{S}_{t+\Delta t} / \partial \mathbf{S}_t acquires an eigenvalue approaching unity in magnitude, and the variance of counterfactual outcomes

\operatorname{Var}\bigl( \mathbf{R}(t+\Delta t) \mid d_{v^*}(t) \bigr)

grows exponentially in \Delta t. Consequently, the mutual information I(d_{v^*}(t); \mathbf{R}(t+\Delta t)) falls below any positive foresight threshold \eta, violating Foreseeability. Assigning liability to a human supervisor to restore Attributability forces \lambda \to 0, which degrades R_\delta above R_H^*, violating Completeness. The four properties are therefore jointly unsatisfiable at a single locus. \blacksquare

H.4 Distributed liability

When \Lambda > \hat{\Lambda}^*, liability is apportioned across the interaction graph proportionally to the directional mutual information flows:

\ell(v) \propto I(D_v; \mathbf{R} \mid V \setminus \{v\}).

Normalizing over V gives a distributed liability vector \ell \in \Delta(V).

