Appendix I: κ-Measurability and Ambiguity-Sensitive Rules

I.1 Reduction map and σ-algebra

Let \pi_1: \Delta(\Delta(\mathcal{R})) \to \Delta(\mathcal{R}) be defined by \pi_1(\nu) = \int p \, d\nu(p). Let

\sigma(\pi_1) \triangleq \{ \pi_1^{-1}(B) : B \in \mathcal{B}(\Delta(\mathcal{R})) \}.

I.2 Measurability classification

· \delta \in \mathcal{M}(\sigma(\pi_1)) iff \delta(\nu) depends on \nu only through \pi_1(\nu).
· \delta \notin \mathcal{M}(\sigma(\pi_1)) iff there exist \nu_1, \nu_2 with \pi_1(\nu_1) = \pi_1(\nu_2) but \delta(\nu_1) \neq \delta(\nu_2).

I.3 SEU violates κ-measurability

Proposition I.1. The Subjective Expected Utility rule

\delta_{\text{SEU}}(\nu) = \arg\min_d \int L(d, \mathbf{R}) \, d(\pi_1(\nu))(\mathbf{R})

satisfies \delta_{\text{SEU}} \in \mathcal{M}(\sigma(\pi_1)).

Proof. By construction, \delta_{\text{SEU}}(\nu) depends on \nu only via \pi_1(\nu). Any two \nu_1, \nu_2 with identical \pi_1 yield identical \delta_{\text{SEU}}. \blacksquare

I.4 Γ-maximin satisfies κ-measurability

Proposition I.2. Let \mathcal{N}_1, \mathcal{N}_2 \subseteq \Delta(\mathcal{R}) with \pi_1(\mathcal{N}_1) = \pi_1(\mathcal{N}_2) = p_0 but \mathcal{N}_1 \subsetneq \mathcal{N}_2. Then for non-constant convex loss L,

\delta_\Gamma(\mathcal{N}_1) \neq \delta_\Gamma(\mathcal{N}_2).

Proof. Since \mathcal{N}_1 \subsetneq \mathcal{N}_2,

\sup_{p \in \mathcal{N}_1} \mathbb{E}_p[L(d, \mathbf{R})] \le \sup_{p \in \mathcal{N}_2} \mathbb{E}_p[L(d, \mathbf{R})]

for every d, with strict inequality for some d when L is non-constant and convex. The minimizers therefore diverge in general. \blacksquare

I.5 KMM satisfies κ-measurability

Proposition I.3. Let \phi be strictly concave and twice continuously differentiable. Then

\delta_{\text{KMM}}(\nu) = \arg\max_d \int \phi\bigl( \mathbb{E}_p[u(d, \mathbf{R})] \bigr) \, d\nu(p)

depends on the second-order variance \operatorname{Var}_\nu(\mathbb{E}_p[u(d, \mathbf{R})]).

Proof. Let v_p = \mathbb{E}_p[u(d, \mathbf{R})] and \bar{v} = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_1(\nu)}[v_p]. Taylor expansion:

\int \phi(v_p) \, d\nu(p) = \phi(\bar{v}) + \tfrac{1}{2} \phi''(\bar{v}) \operatorname{Var}_\nu(v_p) + \mathcal{O}\bigl( \mathbb{E}_\nu[(v_p - \bar{v})^3] \bigr).

Since \phi''(\bar{v}) < 0, the objective strictly penalizes \operatorname{Var}_\nu(v_p). Two states with equal \bar{v} but unequal variance yield distinct objectives, hence \delta_{\text{KMM}} \notin \mathcal{M}(\sigma(\pi_1)). \blacksquare

