Appendix K: Estimation Protocols

K.1 Estimating \rho

Provenance graphs assign each agent a a root r = \phi(a). On gold-labeled cases with known \Theta, compute empirical errors \hat{\varepsilon}_a = e_a - \Theta. Within each root r, estimate the intraclass correlation

\hat{\rho}_r = \frac{\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\bar{\varepsilon}_r) - \widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\varepsilon_a) / |\mathcal{A}_r|}{\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\bar{\varepsilon}_r) + (|\mathcal{A}_r| - 1) \widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\varepsilon_a) / |\mathcal{A}_r|}.

Pool across roots using inverse-variance weights:

\hat{\rho} = \frac{\sum_r w_r \hat{\rho}_r}{\sum_r w_r}, \qquad w_r = \frac{|\mathcal{A}_r|}{\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\hat{\rho}_r)}.

K.2 Estimating \lambda

Identify natural experiments where h and \bar{e} diverge. Fit the linear regression

D = (1-\lambda)h + \lambda \bar{e} + \eta, \qquad \mathbb{E}[\eta \mid h, \bar{e}] = 0.

The coefficient on \bar{e} estimates \lambda under exogeneity of the divergence. Automation-bias studies suggest \lambda varies with AI confidence reports and operator expertise; conditional estimation on these covariates is recommended.

K.3 Estimating n_{\text{eff}}^I

Compute empirical mutual information via k-nearest-neighbor estimators (Kraskov–Stögbauer–Grassberger) or via parametric approximations when Gaussianity is plausible. Report n_{\text{eff}}^I = k \hat{I}(E; \Theta) / \sum_a \hat{I}(e_a; \Theta) with bootstrap confidence intervals.

