Bibliography

Social Choice and Multicriteria Decision Theory

Arrow, K. J. (1951). Social Choice and Individual Values. New York: John Wiley & Sons. (Revised edition, 1963.)

Arrow, K. J., & Raynaud, H. (1986). Social Choice and Multicriterion Decision-Making. Cambridge, MA: MIT Press. This monograph established the formal relationship between social choice theory and multiple-criteria decision analysis, showing that the Arrow–Raynaud conditions render scalar aggregation impossible under ordinal non-comparability.

Survey Methodology and Design Effects

Kish, L. (1965). Survey Sampling. New York: John Wiley & Sons. Kish introduced the design effect (deff) as the ratio of the variance of an estimator under a complex design to the variance under simple random sampling. The design-effect correction deff = 1 + (M − 1)ICC, where M is the average cluster size and ICC is the intracluster correlation coefficient, is the standard form used throughout this model.

Higgins, J. P. T., & Green, S. (Eds.). (2011). Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions (Version 5.1.0). The Cochrane Collaboration. Chapter 16.3.4 specifies that the effective sample size of a cluster-randomized trial is its original sample size divided by the design effect 1 + (M − 1)ICC.

Decision Theory Under Ambiguity

Klibanoff, P., Marinacci, M., & Mukerji, S. (2005). A smooth model of decision making under ambiguity. Econometrica, 73(6), 1849–1892. The KMM model achieves a separation between ambiguity (a characteristic of subjective beliefs) and ambiguity attitude (a characteristic of tastes), with ambiguity aversion characterized by the concavity of the transformation function φ. The maxmin expected utility model of Gilboa and Schmeidler (1989) is a limiting case of the KMM model with infinite ambiguity aversion.

Gilboa, I., & Schmeidler, D. (1989). Maxmin expected utility with non-unique prior. Journal of Mathematical Economics, 18(2), 141–153. The MEU model posits that decision-makers hold a set of prior probabilities and choose acts maximizing the minimum expected utility across that set.

Marinacci, M. (2002). Probabilistic sophistication and multiple priors. Econometrica, 70(2), 755–764. Marinacci introduced and axiomatically characterized the α-maxmin expected utility (α-MEU) criterion, which generalizes the MEU model.

Epistemic Sybil Resistance and Multi-Agent Inference

Bara, M. (2026). Epistemic Sybil resistance: Multiplying AI agents without multiplying evidence. arXiv:2609.01873. A report Z is an epistemic Sybil extension relative to reports R when I(Θ; Z | R) = 0. No report-only aggregator can generally distinguish replication from independent corroboration. Holding one evidence root fixed while report multiplicity rises from 1 to 32 collapses naive posterior coverage from 0.940 to 0.263. The agent's replicate extraction errors are correlated at γ̂_cal = 0.719, estimated out of sample.

Accountability and Governance of Human–AI Collectives

Tibebu, H. (2026). The accountability horizon: An impossibility theorem for governing human-agent collectives. arXiv:2604.07778. The Accountability Incompleteness Theorem proves that for any collective whose compound autonomy exceeds the Accountability Horizon Λ̂* = 1 − 1/|C_min| and whose interaction graph contains a human–AI feedback cycle, no framework can satisfy Attributability, Foreseeability Bound, Non-Vacuity, and Completeness simultaneously. The impossibility is structural: transparency, audits, and oversight cannot resolve it without reducing autonomy.

Child Development and Children's Rights

Committee on the Rights of the Child. (2013). General Comment No. 14: The right of the child to have his or her best interest taken as a primary consideration (CRC/C/GC/14). Paragraph 44 states that the evolving capacities of the child (art. 5) must be taken into consideration when the child's best interests and right to be heard are at stake. As the child matures, his or her views shall have increasing weight in the assessment of his or her best interests.

Fisher Information and Correlated Neural Coding

Zohary, E., Shadlen, M. N., & Newsome, W. T. (1994). Correlated neuronal discharge rate and its implications for psychophysical performance. Nature, 370(6485), 140–143. This study demonstrated that shared variability among neurons cannot be averaged away over the population, limiting the improvement in coding accuracy expected from pooling across neurons. Average noise correlations range from 0.1 to 0.4 in cortical populations, providing the empirical basis for the correlation parameter ρ in the exposure law.

Automation Bias and Human Deference to AI

Deference to AI advice ranges from 0.13 to 0.19 across conditions, while automation bias (over-reliance on incorrect AI) ranges from 0.27 to 0.36. The confidence group showed the lowest odds of automation bias (OR = 0.23, 95% CI: [0.17, ...]), suggesting that deference λ varies systematically with AI confidence displays and operator expertise.

Institutional Governance and Stability

MARIA OS. (2026). Institutional design for agentic societies: Meta-governance theory and AI constitutional frameworks. MARIA OS Blog. The stability condition for governance loops requires spectral radius ρ(J_F) < 1, ensuring bounded evolution. Adaptive institutional frameworks reduce the maximum spectral radius from 1.14 (unstable, unbounded divergence) to 0.82 (stable, bounded evolution). The exact contraction condition is (1 − D) · λ_max(A) < 1, which is the conservative form of the stability law.

Child-Centred AI Governance

UNICEF. (2021). Policy Guidance on AI for Children. New York: UNICEF. The guidance establishes requirements for child-centred AI, including regulatory frameworks and oversight, safety for children, protection of children's data and privacy, non-discrimination and fairness, and transparency, explainability and accountability. The Child Rights by Design framework sets out core principles for integrating child safety into digital and AI systems.

Foundational Decision Theory

Kahneman, D., & Tversky, A. (1979). Prospect theory: An analysis of decision under risk. Econometrica, 47(2), 263–292. Prospect theory was proposed as a descriptive theory of decision-making under risk, offering an alternative to expected utility theory and demonstrating systematic deviations from normative rationality.